--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CHANGES (Apache-2.0 section 4(b): prominent notices stating what changed)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

All changes below were made by brs-holding for the SWARM testnet. Every
modified source file also carries an inline comment marked "SWARM change"
explaining the reason at the point of the change.

1. RPC transport and authentication
   - Added lib/zcash_explorer/rpc.ex. The upstream Zcashex GenServer captured
     a fixed RPC username and password at boot. Zebra authenticates with a
     cookie file whose secret is regenerated on every node start, so the new
     module reads the credential at request time and re-reads it once after an
     authentication failure. The secret is never logged.
   - Removed the Zcashex process from the supervision tree; its decoders are
     still used.
   - config/releases.exs replaced by config/runtime.exs. ZCASHD_USERNAME and
     ZCASHD_PASSWORD are no longer required or read.

2. Features removed because Zebra does not serve them
   - Payment Disclosure (/payment-disclosure): needed the zcashd wallet RPC
     z_validatepaymentdisclosure.
   - Viewing Key (/vk, /api/vk/:hostname, VkLive): shelled out to
     "docker create nighthawkapps/vkrunner" through MuonTrap. The muontrap
     dependency was removed with it.
   - The /price route, which referenced a PriceLive module that does not exist
     in this tree. SWARM test coins have no price and no price is fetched.
   - The "Commitments" tile, which Zebra always reports as 0.
   - The Tor onion-address link and config/onion_address.txt.

3. Network identity and the block-reward allocation
   - Added lib/zcash_explorer/swarm.ex. Network name, ticker, maximum supply,
     halving interval, block target and the three reward destinations all come
     from configuration; nothing is hard-coded per deployment.
   - The network profile. One image serves both networks: ZcashExplorer.Swarm
     .network_kind/0 reads SWARM_NETWORK_KIND or, failing that, the network
     name, and the "test coins have no value" wording in the hero, the footer
     and the not-found page is shown only on a test network. An unrecognised
     network name is treated as a test network, so the warning cannot be
     dropped by a misconfiguration.
   - The allocation is read in either shape it is written in: the bare array
     this explorer has always taken, and the {"recipients": [...]} object with
     "numerator" that the SWARM stack's own renderer writes beside the node's
     configuration, so one file can serve both and they cannot drift apart.
   - The block page gained a "Block reward" breakdown: four rows (miner plus
     the three allocations) with amount, share of the subsidy and a linked
     destination address, read from getblocksubsidy.
   - Zebra's upstream funding-stream slot names are relabelled for display:
     ECC / "Electric Coin Company" -> Core Development;
     MajorGrants / "Major Grants" / "Zcash Community Grants NU6" ->
     Grants & Ecosystem;
     ZcashFoundation / "Zcash Foundation" -> Community & Development Reserve.
   - The address page badges an address that is one of the three destinations.
   - The home page gained total supply issued against the maximum, the next
     halving height, the network solution rate and every shielded value pool;
     the fixed Orchard-only tile became ShieldedPoolLive.

4. Address routing
   - Added the testnet human-readable prefixes ztestsapling (Sapling) and
     utest (unified) to the shielded and unified routing. Upstream only knew
     the mainnet prefixes zc, zs and u, so a SwarmTestnet shielded address was
     sent to the transparent code path.

5. Cache warmers
   - Added lib/zcash_explorer/warmer_window.ex. The window is clamped at height
     0, so a chain shorter than the window (including heights 0-10) no longer
     asks the node for negative heights, and the refresh interval is derived
     from the configured block target (25 s for 75-second blocks) instead of a
     fixed 15 s.
   - The Cachex bound was reduced from 10,000 to 1,500 entries by default with
     a 15-minute expiry, both env-configurable, for a 2 vCPU / 4 GB server.

6. Database
   - Removed ZcashExplorer.Repo, priv/repo, the ecto_sql, postgrex,
     phoenix_ecto and ecto_psql_extras dependencies and the Ecto SQL sandbox
     from the test cases. The repository was already absent from the
     supervision tree and nothing queried it, but its presence meant the test
     suite could not run without a Postgres database.

7. Branding, design system and privacy
   - Nighthawk Apps branding, the @ZcashExplorer link, the mainnet/testnet
     cross-links and the Zcash logo were replaced with SWARM's own.
   - The interface follows the Swarm Style Guide v2: warm black #0A0908 with a
     faint hexagon field, surfaces #100E0C / #171411, Hive Orange #FF8A1F for
     brand and SHIELDED state, honey #FFB020 for COINBASE and rewards, Clear
     Blue #6FB6FF reserved strictly for transparent ("REVEALED") data, green
     #3DD68C for confirmations. The light theme and its localStorage toggle
     were removed; the explorer now stores nothing in the browser beyond the
     session cookie Phoenix needs.
   - The Google Fonts stylesheet link was removed. Sora, Manrope and JetBrains
     Mono are bundled from npm (@fontsource) and served from this origin, so
     the page makes no third-party request of any kind. No analytics, no
     trackers, no third-party scripts.
   - New: a hero with the search box, eight metric cards with the flowing top
     edge, a "Latest blocks" list with a LIVE pill and a per-block shielded
     share, a "Latest transactions" list and a block transaction list with
     SHIELDED / REVEALED / COINBASE state pills.
   - Shielded amounts render as the masked glyphs and never as a number: the
     chain holds no public amount for such a transaction, and printing its
     transparent total would describe something other than what was sent.
   - The per-block shielded share is computed from the block's own non-coinbase
     transactions (BlockView.shielded_share/1) and is nil, shown as an em dash,
     for a block that holds nothing but its coinbase.
   - Figures the node cannot supply are em dashes, never placeholders. In
     particular getnetworksolps returns 0 on a minimum-difficulty chain, so a
     non-positive reading shows an em dash; the card is labelled Sol/s because
     Equihash is measured in solutions, never in hashes per second.
   - The design mockup's "NODES / countries" card was not implemented. This
     explorer talks to one node and cannot see the size of the network, so the
     card shows the peer count of that node, labelled "peers of this node".
   - The coin ticker is SWM. The project is SWARM.
   - A footer states "SWARM TESTNET - TEST COINS HAVE NO VALUE" and the
     non-affiliation above.
   - check_origin and the endpoint URL are derived from EXPLORER_HOSTNAME.

8. Operations
   - Dockerfile rewritten: multi-stage, non-root (uid 10001, matching the
     official zfnd/zebra image so the RPC cookie can be read from a shared
     volume without being world-readable), HEALTHCHECK against a new /healthz
     endpoint that makes no RPC call, BEAM schedulers pinned to two in
     rel/vm.args.eex for a small shared-CPU server.
   - Added .github/workflows/swarm-ci.yml, which compiles with warnings as
     errors, runs the test suite, builds the image, mines a Regtest chain with
     a real Zebra 6.3.0 node, asserts the pages against it, restarts the node
     to prove the explorer survives a cookie rotation, and saves the image as
     a tarball artifact.
   - Renamed ".env.example " (with a trailing space) to ".env.example", which
     is what made the repository impossible to check out on Windows.

9. Phoenix secrets are read from the environment, never compiled in
   - config/config.exs set secret_key_base and the LiveView signing_salt, and
     lib/zcash_explorer_web/endpoint.ex set the session signing_salt in a
     module attribute. All three are compile-time, so every build of this fork
     froze upstream's published development values into the release's
     sys.config and into the beam, where whoever ran the image could not
     replace them.
   - config/runtime.exs now reads SECRET_KEY_BASE, SESSION_SIGNING_SALT and
     LIVE_VIEW_SIGNING_SALT on every boot and raises when one is missing or
     empty, so a misconfigured deployment stops instead of quietly signing with
     a value published in a public repository. Throwaway values for a local run
     live in config/dev.exs and config/test.exs, which no release reads.
   - The endpoint's session options moved into ZcashExplorerWeb.Endpoint
     .session_options/0, which the session plug and the LiveView socket both
     read, so the two cannot drift apart.
   - ci/check-release-secrets.py decodes Erlang byte runs and compares as
     bytes, because the salt appears in sys.config as <<97,52,...>> and a grep
     for its text finds nothing there — which reads like evidence of absence
     and is not. CI runs it against the sys.config inside the image it just
     built, and proves the image refuses to start with any one of the three
     variables missing.

10. Search routing
   - The search box asked the node four questions in parallel and redirected to
     whichever answered first, block first. A failed call decoded as a
     successful nil (see below), so every query was sent to /blocks/<query>: a
     transparent address reached the block page, which cannot parse one, and
     the visitor got HTTP 500. On this chain the three published allocation
     addresses are the likeliest thing anyone searches for.
   - ZcashExplorer.Rpc.decode/1 matched {"result": ..., "error": %{}} meaning
     "an empty error object", but %{} in a pattern matches any map, so Zebra's
     error replies were reported to callers as {:ok, nil}. It now tests the
     map's size, and an error reply is an error at every call site.
   - ZcashExplorerWeb.SearchController classifies a query by shape, which needs
     no node: digits are a height, 64 hex characters are a block hash or a
     transaction id (the one case the node is still asked to resolve), and the
     transparent, shielded and unified prefixes go to their own pages. The
     charset is checked as well as the prefix, because the query reaches a
     Location header.
   - Anything unrecognised renders a not-found page
     (templates/error/invalid_input.html.heex) that says what the box accepts.
     It replaced the string "Invalid input", and the block and address routes
     render it too, so no search can end in a 500.
